<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Esan chiefdoms</h1><p class="st-meta">1460 CE – 1897 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A cluster of small Edo-speaking kingdoms north-east of Benin City, each ruled by an Onojie under the influence of the Benin Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Esan tradition links the founding of most chiefdoms to the 15th century, when people left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare</a> and settled in the forest to the north-east. Princes and nobles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onojie">Enijie</a> (singular Onojie), founding chiefdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uromi">Uromi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekpoma">Ekpoma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrua">Irrua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubiaja">Ubiaja</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each chiefdom had its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onojie">Onojie</a>, chiefs and age grades, and ruled villages of yam and palm farmers. The Enijie owed allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba of Benin</a>, sent tribute and took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin's</a> wars, but fought one another frequently. Esan shared much of Edo religion and court culture.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the British conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> in 1897, the Esan chiefdoms were occupied; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uromi">Uromi</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onojie">Onojie</a> Okolo resisted until 1901. They became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Southern Nigeria</a>, and the Enijie remain traditional rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-state">Edo State</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1460 CE – 1897 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.76%;width:8.70%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1460 CE – 1897 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Okolo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Edo traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Esan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ishan chiefdoms, Esan kingdoms, Esanland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=esan-chiefdoms" aria-label="Esan chiefdoms on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4883 1829 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Esan chiefdoms lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5155 2197h0M5175 2243h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5203 2085h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5203 2085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Nigeria. <a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=esan-chiefdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

